window.docContents[120].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Bimodules-and-tensor-products___-associativity-and-Hom-tensor-adjunction/#Chapter2___Problem2___11___6":{"contents":"Problem 2.11.6. Throughout this problem, we let k be an arbitrary field (not necessarily of characteristic zero and not necessarily algebraically closed).\n\nIf A and B are two k-algebras, then an (A, B)-bimodule will mean a k-vector space V with both a left A-module structure and a right B-module structure which satisfy (av) b = a (vb) for any v \\in V, a \\in A, and b \\in B. Note that both the notions of \"left A-module\" and \"right A-module\" are particular cases of the notion of bimodules; namely, a left A-module is the same as an (A, k)-bimodule, and a right A-module is the same as a (k, A)-bimodule.\n\nLet B be a k-algebra, W a left B-module, and V a right B-module. We denote by V \\otimes_B W the k-vector space (V \\otimes_k W) / \\langle vb \\otimes w - v \\otimes bw \\mid v \\in V, w \\in W, b \\in B \\rangle. We denote the projection of a pure tensor v \\otimes w (with v \\in V and w \\in W) onto the space V \\otimes_B W by v \\otimes_B w. (Note that this tensor product V \\otimes_B W is the one defined in Remark 2.11.4.)\n\nIf, additionally, A is another k-algebra and if the right B-module structure on V is part of an (A, B)-bimodule structure, then V \\otimes_B W becomes a left A-module by a (v \\otimes_B w) = av \\otimes_B w for any a \\in A, v \\in V, and w \\in W.\n\nSimilarly, if C is another k-algebra, and if the left B-module structure on W is part of a (B, C)-bimodule structure, then V \\otimes_B W becomes a right C-module by (v \\otimes_B w) c = v \\otimes_B wc for any c \\in C, v \\in V, and w \\in W.\nIf V is an (A, B)-bimodule and W is a (B, C)-bimodule, then these two structures on V \\otimes_B W can be combined into one (A, C)-bimodule structure on V \\otimes_B W.\n\n(a) Let A, B, C, D be four algebras. Let V be an (A, B)-bimodule, W a (B, C)-bimodule, and X a (C, D)-bimodule. Prove that (V \\otimes_B W) \\otimes_C X \\cong V \\otimes_B (W \\otimes_C X) as (A, D)-bimodules. The isomorphism (from left to right) is given by the formula\n\n\n(v \\otimes_B w) \\otimes_C x \\mapsto v \\otimes_B (w \\otimes_C x)\n\n\nfor all v \\in V, w \\in W, and x \\in X.\n\n(b) If A, B, C are three algebras and if V is an (A, B)-bimodule and W an (A, C)-bimodule, then the vector space \\operatorname{Hom}_A(V, W) (the space of all left A-linear homomorphisms from V to W) canonically becomes a (B, C)-bimodule by setting (bf)(v) = f(vb) for all b \\in B, f \\in \\operatorname{Hom}_A(V, W), and v \\in V and setting (fc)(v) = f(v) c for all c \\in C, f \\in \\operatorname{Hom}_A(V, W) and v \\in V.\n\nLet A, B, C, D be four algebras. Let V be a (B, A)-bimodule, W a (C, B)-bimodule, and X a (C, D)-bimodule. Prove that\n\n\n\\operatorname{Hom}_B(V, \\operatorname{Hom}_C(W, X)) \\cong \\operatorname{Hom}_C(W \\otimes_B V, X)\n\n\nas (A, D)-bimodules. The isomorphism (from left to right) is given by\n\n\nf \\mapsto (w \\otimes_B v \\mapsto f(v) w)\n\n\nfor all v \\in V, w \\in W and f \\in \\operatorname{Hom}_B(V, \\operatorname{Hom}_C(W, X)).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.11. Tensor products\u0009Bimodules and tensor products: associativity and Hom-tensor adjunction","header":"Bimodules and tensor products: associativity and Hom-tensor adjunction","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Bimodules-and-tensor-products___-associativity-and-Hom-tensor-adjunction/#Chapter2___Problem2___11___6"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Irreducible-and-indecomposable-quiver-representations/#Chapter2___Discussion_quiver_irreducible_indecomposable":{"contents":"As with representations of algebras, a nonzero representation (V_i) of a quiver Q is said to be irreducible if its only subrepresentations are (0) and (V_i) itself, and it is said to be indecomposable if it is not isomorphic to a direct sum of two nonzero representations.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Irreducible and indecomposable quiver representations","header":"Irreducible and indecomposable quiver representations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Irreducible-and-indecomposable-quiver-representations/#Chapter2___Discussion_quiver_irreducible_indecomposable"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Concrete-Lie-algebra-examples-_LPAR_continued_RPAR____-aff_LPAR_1_RPAR____-so_LPAR_n_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Discussion_concrete_Lie_examples_continued___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Concrete Lie algebra examples (continued): aff(1), so(n)\u0009Concrete Lie algebra examples (continued): aff(1), so(n)","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Concrete-Lie-algebra-examples-_LPAR_continued_RPAR____-aff_LPAR_1_RPAR____-so_LPAR_n_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Discussion_concrete_Lie_examples_continued___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_--Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"A Lie algebra equivalence between the displayed Lie algebras.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_concrete_Lie_examples/Derived3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.9.12; role=primary]\n\nThe displayed matrices are equal.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_concrete_Lie_examples/Derived3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.9.12; role=primary]\n\nThe displayed matrices are equal.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_concrete_Lie_examples/Derived3; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.9.12; role=primary]\n\nThe displayed type is nontrivial.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Example2.9.12; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Universal enveloping algebra of sl(2)\u0009Universal enveloping algebra of sl(2)\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_--Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Canonical-decomposition-of-semisimple-representations-via-Schur___s-lemma/#Chapter3___Remark3___1___3":{"contents":"Remark 3.1.3. Note that by Schur's lemma, any semisimple finite dimensional representation V of A is canonically identified with \\bigoplus_X \\operatorname{Hom}_A(X, V) \\otimes X, where X runs over all irreducible representations of A. Indeed, we have a natural map f : \\bigoplus_X \\operatorname{Hom}(X, V) \\otimes X \\to V, given by g \\otimes x \\to g(x), x \\in X, g \\in \\operatorname{Hom}(X, V), and it is easy to verify that this map is an isomorphism. Indeed, if the result holds for representations V_i for i \\in I, then it holds for their direct sum. Therefore one may assume that V is irreducible.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Canonical decomposition of semisimple representations via Schur's lemma","header":"Canonical decomposition of semisimple representations via Schur's lemma","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Canonical-decomposition-of-semisimple-representations-via-Schur___s-lemma/#Chapter3___Remark3___1___3"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___2___-The-density-theorem/Any-linear-map-on-linearly-independent-vectors-is-realized-by-an-algebra-element/#Chapter3___Corollary3___2___1":{"contents":"Corollary 3.2.1. Let V be an irreducible finite dimensional representation of A, and let v_1, \\ldots, v_n \\in V be any linearly independent vectors. Then for any w_1, \\ldots, w_n \\in V there exists an element a \\in A such that av_i = w_i for all i.\n\nProof. Assume the contrary. Then the image of the map A \\to nV given by a \\mapsto (av_1, \\ldots, av_n) is a proper subrepresentation, so by Proposition 3.1.4 it corresponds to an r \\times n matrix X, r < n. Thus, taking a = 1, we see that there exist vectors u_1, \\ldots, u_r \\in V such that (u_1, \\ldots, u_r)X = (v_1, \\ldots, v_n). Let (q_1, \\ldots, q_n) be a nonzero vector such that X(q_1, \\ldots, q_n)^T = 0 (it exists because r < n). Then \\sum q_i v_i = (u_1, \\ldots, u_r)X(q_1, \\ldots, q_n)^T = 0, i.e. \\sum q_i v_i = 0 — a contradiction to the linear independence of v_i. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.2. The density theorem\u0009Any linear map on linearly independent vectors is realized by an algebra element","header":"Any linear map on linearly independent vectors is realized by an algebra element","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___2___-The-density-theorem/Any-linear-map-on-linearly-independent-vectors-is-realized-by-an-algebra-element/#Chapter3___Corollary3___2___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Character-of-U_lambda-as-coefficient-of-x___lambda-in-product-of-power-sums/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___--Character-of-U_lambda-as-coefficient-of-x___lambda-in-product-of-power-sums--Character-of-U_lambda-as-coefficient-of-x___lambda-in-product-of-power-sums--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"For an index at least two, the auxiliary value is the multiplicity of that index in the permutation's cycle type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.14.3; role=primary]\n\nAt index one, the auxiliary value is the cardinality of the fixed-point type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.14.3; role=primary]\n\nConnects the auxiliary numerical value with the indicated polynomial coefficient.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.14.3; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.14. Induced representations for $S_n$\u0009Character of U_lambda as coefficient of x^lambda in product of power sums\u0009Character of U_lambda as coefficient of x^lambda in product of power sums\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Character-of-U_lambda-as-coefficient-of-x___lambda-in-product-of-power-sums/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___--Character-of-U_lambda-as-coefficient-of-x___lambda-in-product-of-power-sums--Character-of-U_lambda-as-coefficient-of-x___lambda-in-product-of-power-sums--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Artin___s-theorem___-equivalence-of-element-coverage-and-character-span/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___26___-Artin___s-theorem--Artin___s-theorem___-equivalence-of-element-coverage-and-character-span--Artin___s-theorem___-equivalence-of-element-coverage-and-character-span--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"For a conjugation-stable family of subgroups, the auxiliary covering condition is equivalent to character membership in the displayed span.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.26.1; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.26.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.26. Artin's theorem\u0009Artin's theorem: equivalence of element coverage and character span\u0009Artin's theorem: equivalence of element coverage and character span\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Artin___s-theorem___-equivalence-of-element-coverage-and-character-span/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___26___-Artin___s-theorem--Artin___s-theorem___-equivalence-of-element-coverage-and-character-span--Artin___s-theorem___-equivalence-of-element-coverage-and-character-span--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Section-6___8-heading-and-setup-for-proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/#Chapter6___Section6___8_heading___heading-1":{"contents":"Let V be an indecomposable representation of Q. We introduce a fixed labeling 1, \\ldots, n on Q, such that i < j if one can reach j from i. This is possible, since we can assign the highest label to any sink, remove this sink from the quiver, assign the next highest label to a sink of the remaining quiver, and so on. This way we create a labeling of the desired kind.\n\nWe now consider the sequence\n\nV^{(0)} = V, \\quad V^{(1)} = F_n^+ V, \\quad V^{(2)} = F_{n-1}^+ F_n^+ V, \\quad \\ldots.\n\nThis sequence is well defined because of the selected labeling: n has to be a sink of Q, n-1 has to be a sink of \\overline{Q}_n (where \\overline{Q}_r is obtained from Q by reversing all the arrows at the vertex r) and so on. Furthermore, we note that V^{(n)} is a representation of Q again, since every arrow has been reversed twice (since we applied a reflection functor to every vertex). This implies that we can define\n\nV^{(n+1)} = F_n^+ V^{(n)}, \\ldots\n\nand continue the sequence to infinity.\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.8. Proof of Gabriel's theorem\u0009Section 6.8 heading and setup for proof of Gabriel's theorem\u0009Section 6.8 heading and setup for proof of Gabriel's theorem","header":"6.8. Proof of Gabriel's theorem","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Section-6___8-heading-and-setup-for-proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/#Chapter6___Section6___8_heading___heading-1"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Yoneda-Lemma/#Chapter7___Lemma7___5___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.5. Representable functors\u0009Yoneda Lemma\u0009Yoneda Lemma","header":"Formalization","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Yoneda-Lemma/#Chapter7___Lemma7___5___1___formalization"}});